---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import NavBar from "../components/NavBar.astro";

const items = [
  {
    q: "Is ShipGlows a server tool or an AI workflow framework?",
    a: "Neither label captures the product hierarchy. ShipGlows is a business-aware delivery partner first. Agent workflows, governed context, verification, environments, and server operations are capabilities used to carry that promise through proof."
  },
  {
    q: "What does business-aware mean in practice?",
    a: "ShipGlows connects a request to applicable business, product, market, and brand truth before choosing a solution. It can challenge weak framing, explain material trade-offs, and recommend a direction instead of optimizing only for the easiest technical move."
  },
  {
    q: "Does ‘business partner’ mean consulting or human services?",
    a: "No. The partnership is software behavior: ShipGlows uses governed context, useful judgment, guided choices, outcome ownership, and proof. It does not offer consulting, diagnostics, implementation missions, or human accompaniment. A possible future Cockpit SaaS does not exist and is not an offer today."
  },
  {
    q: "Does ShipGlows make business decisions for me?",
    a: "No. It researches discoverable facts, proposes useful interpretations, and asks focused questions when intent, promise, appetite, cost, or authority belongs to you. It does not claim that its advice is always correct or guarantee market, growth, revenue, or conversion outcomes."
  },
  {
    q: "Do all skills work like free-text prompts?",
    a: "No. You can describe many jobs in plain language, but some workflows need a precise mode or structured input so the agent can take the right path. The skill contract explains when an argument is task text, a mode switch, or structured input."
  },
  {
    q: "Do ShipGlows skills work with both Codex and Claude Code?",
    a: "Yes. You can use the same public workflow vocabulary in both environments. ShipGlows maintains the discovery metadata each runtime needs and keeps the shared skill descriptions compact and audited against the stricter common budget.",
    links: [
      ["Codex skills documentation", "https://developers.openai.com/codex/skills"],
      ["Claude Code skills documentation", "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills"]
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "How do I install ShipGlows in Codex?",
    a: "You can reach a first workflow without assembling the system by hand. Add the ShipGlows marketplace source with `codex plugin marketplace add dianedef/ShipGlows --ref main --sparse .agents/plugins --sparse plugins/shipglows`, restart Codex, open the plugin directory, install `shipglows` from the ShipGlows marketplace, then start with `$shipglows help me choose the right workflow`."
  },
  {
    q: "What is a skill mode?",
    a: "A mode helps a skill recognize the kind of result you need without making you describe its whole procedure. Technically, it is a specialized workflow selected by an argument pattern or keyword; for example, a docs skill can enter metadata-migration mode instead of normal documentation writing."
  },
  {
    q: "Which workflow should I use when I need evidence from a browser?",
    a: "Choose the narrowest proof path for the question you actually need answered. Use `sg-browser` for one non-auth browser check such as visible state, layout, screenshot, console, or network evidence; `sg-auth-debug` for login, OAuth, cookies, sessions, callbacks, tenants, providers, or protected routes; `sg-test` for guided manual QA and durable test or bug records; and `sg-prod` when the deployment URL, runtime logs, or live deploy state are still uncertain.",
    links: [
      ["sg-browser", "/skills/sg-browser"],
      ["sg-auth-debug", "/skills/sg-auth-debug"],
      ["sg-test", "/skills/sg-test"],
      ["sg-prod", "/skills/sg-prod"]
    ]
  },
  {
    q: "Why not just prompt agents harder?",
    a: "Because a stronger prompt still leaves you to reconstruct context, judge unclear scope, and notice drift after the fact. ShipGlows makes the handoff more explicit through context, task contracts, and verification instead of relying on one increasingly elaborate instruction."
  },
  {
    q: "Does ShipGlows choose the fastest path by default?",
    a: "No. It aims to help you avoid a fast-looking path that leaves an avoidable problem behind. Its decision-quality contract lets speed, cost, and convenience decide only when the relevant options are already equivalent on correctness, security posture, maintainability, performance, and proof."
  },
  {
    q: "Does ShipGlows guarantee secure or bug-free software?",
    a: "No. It makes edge-case, code-quality, and security gates explicit when the scope warrants them. Internet-facing or privileged work uses OWASP Top 10 awareness and selected verifiable controls, but ShipGlows does not claim certification, complete OWASP coverage, or guaranteed security."
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need every ShipGlows document before I can use it?",
    a: "No. You can start with the workflow that fits the job in front of you. Context maps and decision contracts become more useful as work becomes more expensive, ambiguous, or cross-cutting; they are not a prerequisite for every small task."
  },
  {
    q: "Does every Markdown file need ShipGlows frontmatter?",
    a: "No. Ordinary notes can stay lightweight. ShipGlows targets explicit active artifacts rather than every Markdown file in a repository; formalizing a file later is a conscious documentation decision."
  },
  {
    q: "How do I choose between a dependency upgrade and a documentation migration?",
    a: "Use `sg-technical migrate` when you are upgrading a framework or dependency. For metadata or frontmatter migration, use `sg-docs migrate-frontmatter`; these paths keep a technical upgrade separate from documentation structure work."
  },
  {
    q: "Where can I see the status of a spec-first chantier?",
    a: "Open the spec to see what has happened and what remains, without reconstructing the work from chat history. For spec-first work, it records the Skill Run History and Current Chantier Flow, including the lifecycle steps, their result, and the next action."
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need a bug record for a direct fix?",
    a: "Usually, yes when the fix changes real behavior: it gives the next person the diagnosis, repair, and retest evidence instead of leaving it in chat. `sg-fix` should create or reuse a BUG-ID; narrow copy-only, cosmetic-only, or duplicate cases can stay lightweight when the exception is stated."
  },
  {
    q: "Do TASKS.md, AUDIT_LOG.md, or PROJECTS.md store the chantier history?",
    a: "No. You can find the durable story of one chantier in its spec, without reading a general tracker or chat history. TASKS.md, AUDIT_LOG.md, and PROJECTS.md remain operational trackers; the spec carries the per-chantier flow."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="ShipGlows FAQ | Direct answers about workflow, docs, and skills"
  description="Direct answers to the most common ShipGlows questions: what it is, how skills work, and why the framework is structured the way it is."
>
  <main class="page-shell">
    <NavBar />

    <section class="section">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">FAQ</p>
        <h1 class="page-title">The common questions, without the soft-focus version.</h1>
        <p>
          ShipGlows combines business-aware judgment, métier ownership, governed
          execution, and delivery proof. That promise deserves clear boundaries,
          so this page answers the recurring questions directly.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="faq-list">
        {
          items.map((item) => (
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary>{item.q}</summary>
              <p>{item.a}</p>
              {
                "links" in item && (
                  <p>
                    {item.links?.map(([label, href], index) => (
                      <>
                        <a href={href}>{label}</a>
                        {index < (item.links?.length ?? 0) - 1 ? " · " : ""}
                      </>
                    ))}
                  </p>
                )
              }
            </details>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="doc-grid">
        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Want the detailed version?</h3>
          <p>
            If your question is really about how skills interpret arguments,
            start with the launch cheatsheet for master skills, supporting
            skills, and mode detection.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/skill-modes">Read the launch cheatsheet</a>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Want the install path?</h3>
          <p>
            If the question is really how to add ShipGlows to Codex, the install
            page gives the marketplace command and the first-run path.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/install">Open install instructions</a>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Want the structural version?</h3>
          <p>
            If your question is really about documentation roles, context
            routing, and contracts, the docs overview is the better entrypoint.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/docs">Open the docs overview</a>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
          <h3>Want a faster way to steer the AI?</h3>
          <p>
            Use simple fast tags like <code>#partner</code>,
            <code>#growth</code>, <code>#clarity</code>, or
            <code>#shipglows</code> instead of rewriting a long corrective
            prompt.
          </p>
          <a class="doc-action" href="/docs#recommended-combos">Open the focus-tags cheatsheet</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
